# HQL-0064: I/O FAQ

Companion to [[HQL-0064]]. Each entry is a program that hangs, fails or
gives a result its author did not expect. Each entry gives what happens,
the rule behind it, and the program that works. The last section holds the
opposite case: a program that reads as a mistake and is correct. The rules
are those of the
companion notes [child processes](02-child-processes.hmd),
[advanced child processes](03-advanced-child-processes.hmd),
[named pipes](04-named-pipes.hmd) and [`parallel`](05-parallel.hmd).

!!! warning "Design only"
    Nothing in this note is implemented. The programs use the design
    direction of the companion notes.

## Waiting forever

### Why does a `sink` to `sort` never return?

```hql
tap(command("tail", ["-f", "app.log"]), Lines)
| sink(command("sort"), Lines)
```

`sink` closes the child's standard input only when its stream ends. `tail
-f` never ends, so the stream never ends. `sort` writes nothing until it
reads its end of input. Each process waits for the other, and `sink` never
returns.

```d2
direction: right

tail: "tail -f\nnever ends"
sink: "sink\ncloses stdin when the stream ends"
sort: "sort\nwrites when stdin is closed"

tail -> sink: "a line at a time"
sink -> sort: "a line at a time, never closed"
sort -> sink: "waits for end of input" {style.stroke-dash: 3}
```

A child that reads its whole input needs an input that ends. Read a source
that ends, and write it with `sink` or `target`:

```hql
source(file("app.log"), Lines) | sink(command("sort"), Lines)
source(file("app.log"), Lines) | target(command("sort"), Lines)
```

`sink` writes one line for each value and closes standard input after the
last. `target` writes the lines as one document and closes it. Either way
`sort` reads its end of input and answers. `tap(…) | take(1000) | sink(…)`
ends too, since `take` ends the stream.

### Why does `sort` with `stdin_from` never answer?

```hql
names = Channel<String>("names")
["wren", "kite"] | sink(names)
source(command("sort", stdin_from = names), Lines)
```

A wired standard input stays open until the child ends. A channel has no
end, so `sort` never reads an end of input, and `source` waits for a child
that waits for input. A child that reads its whole input is written through
its primary pipe:

```hql
["wren", "kite"] | target(command("sort"), Lines)
```

### Why does a conversation written as two statements hang?

```hql
questions = Channel<String>("questions")
bc = command("bc", ["-l"], stdin_from = questions)

answers = tap(bc, Lines) | take(2)
["2 + 2", "10 / 4"] | sink(questions)
```

Statements run in order. The first reads answers to questions the second
has not written yet, so it never ends, and the second never runs. The read
and the write go in two branches of a `parallel` block, as in the example
"A conversation" of [advanced child processes](03-advanced-child-processes.hmd).

### Why does a `parallel` block with a `tap` never end?

```hql
r = parallel {
    answers = tap(bc, Lines)
    asked   = ["2 + 2"] | sink(questions)
}
```

A branch whose result is a stream is read to its end inside the branch.
`bc` keeps running after its last answer, so the stream does not end and
the block does not end. Bound the read by what the program expects:
`tap(bc, Lines) | take(1)`.

### Why does a `tap` of a channel wait after the child has ended?

```hql
sorted = Channel<String>("sorted")
["wren", "kite"] | target(command("sort", stdout_to = sorted), Lines)
tap(sorted)
```

`tap(c)` gives only what is written after it. `target` returns when `sort`
has ended, so every line is already in the channel and nothing more is
written. The stream waits for a value that never comes. Read what the
channel holds with `source(sorted)`.

### Why does reading a named pipe wait before the first line?

```hql
tap(fifo(Path("/tmp/events")), Lines) | take(1)
```

Opening a named pipe for reading waits until some process opens it for
writing. Nothing is read, and no line arrives, until a writer exists. The
same holds the other way: `sink` to a named pipe waits until a reader opens
it. Start the other side first, or start both in two branches of a
`parallel` block.

## Failing

### Why does a `grep` that matches nothing stop the program?

```hql
tap(git, Lines) | sink(command("grep", ["--line-buffered", "wren"]), Lines)
```

`grep` exits `1` when nothing matches. Every status other than `0` is the
failure `exited`, and under `source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` a failure
stops evaluation. A program that reads the status uses `run` and matches
it:

```hql
match command("grep", ["wren", "names.txt"]).run() {
    ok(out)                    => out.stdout,
    err(e) given e.status == 1 => "",
    err(e)                     => fmt("grep failed: {}", e.stderr),
}
```

### Why does a `target` to `head` sometimes fail with `closed`?

```hql
source(git, Lines) | target(command("head", ["-1"]), Lines)
```

`head -1` reads one line, exits `0`, and closes its standard input. When
the rest of the input no longer fits in the pipe, `hql` is still writing
when the child closes it, and the write fails with the reason `closed`.
Whether the input fits depends on its size and on timing, so the same
program fails on a long log and succeeds on a short one. Take the first
line in HQL instead: `source(git, Lines) | take(1)`.

### Why is `source` of a wired pipe an `invalid-argument` failure?

```hql
out = Channel<String>("out")
source(command("ls", stdout_to = out), Lines)
```

`source` reads standard output as its primary pipe, and `stdout_to` wires
the same pipe to a channel. A pipe is either primary or wired. Drop one:
read the command, or read the channel after a `run`.

### Why is `command("ls *.txt")` the failure `not-found`?

A shell does two things to `ls *.txt` before any program runs. It splits
the line into words, the program `ls` and the argument `*.txt`, and it
replaces `*.txt` with the names of the files that match. No shell reads a
command, so neither happens:

- `command("ls *.txt")` names a program `ls *.txt`, with the space and the
  star. No program has that name, and the reason is `not-found`.
- `command("ls", ["*.txt"])` runs `ls` with the literal argument `*.txt`.
  `ls` does not expand a glob, finds no file of that name, and exits `1`.
  The reason is `exited`.

The expansion is a glob, and `io.fs` has one. Each match becomes one
argument, so a name with a space or a `;` stays one argument:

```hql
txt = folder(working_dir()) | glob(Glob("*.txt")) | map(e => e.name)
command("ls", txt)
```

A program that needs shell text runs a shell, and the project grants it by
name, as the section "The grant" of
[child processes](02-child-processes.hmd) states:

```hql
command("sh", ["-c", "ls *.txt"])
```

### Why does the child not see `HOME`?

A child inherits only the environment variables the `env` grant gives the
program. Under `env = ["PATH"]`, the child sees `PATH` and nothing else.
Grant the variable, or add it to the `env` argument of `command`.

## Results that surprise

### Why does one command run twice?

```hql
git = command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"])
{authors: source(git, Lines) | distinct, commits: size(source(git, Lines))}
```

A `Command` is a description, not a run. Each read or write starts the
program once, so this runs `git log` twice. Bind the result once and read
the binding:

```hql
names = source(git, Lines)
{authors: names | distinct, commits: size(names)}
```

### Why do a child's lines appear in the program's output?

```hql
tap(git, Lines) | sink(command("tr", ["a-z", "A-Z"]), Lines)
```

`sink` writes the child's standard input, its primary pipe. Its standard
output is secondary and goes to this process's standard output, so `tr`
prints its lines directly, and the result of `sink` is a `Step`. To get the
lines as values, wire the output: `command("tr", …, stdout_to = channel)`,
then `source(channel)`.

### Why is a child terminated after `take`?

```hql
tap(command("git", ["log"]), Lines) | take(10)
```

`take(10)` ends the read after ten lines, and `git` is still running. A
child's lifetime is bounded by the evaluation, or the `parallel` branch,
that started it, so `hql` terminates it: `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after 5
seconds. A child that must finish is read with `source`.

### Why are lines from two pipes in a different order on each run?

```hql
log = Channel<String>("log")
command("make", stdout_to = log, stderr_to = log).run()
source(log)
```

Lines of one pipe keep their order. Lines of two pipes are appended in the
order they reach `hql`, and the kernel does not fix an order between two
pipes. Wire each pipe to its own channel when the order matters within it,
and do not depend on an order between them.

## Reading as a mistake, and correct

### Is a list written with `sink` a mistake?

```hql
questions = Channel<String>("questions")
["2 + 2", "10 / 4"] | sink(questions)
source(questions)
```

```text
["2 + 2", "10 / 4"]
```

It reads as one: a list is a batch, and `sink` is the operation that writes
a stream. It is correct, and `target` is the mistake here.

`sink` writes one document for each element of its input, and it accepts
anything that iterates, ended or not:

1. `sink` appends `"2 + 2"` to the channel as one value.
2. It appends `"10 / 4"`.
3. The list has no further element, so `sink` returns a `Step`.

`target` writes its whole input as one document. The channel holds
`String`, and the whole input is a list:

```hql
["2 + 2", "10 / 4"] | target(questions)
```

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: Channel<String> holds String, not List<String>
```

Both programs run today: a channel is implemented.

### What is the difference between `target` and `sink`?

What counts as a document. `target(v)` writes what `sink` writes over the
one-element sequence `[v]`:

| Program | Documents written |
|---|---|
| `["a", "b"] \| target(stdout, Json)` | one: the array `["a", "b"]` |
| `[["a", "b"]] \| sink(stdout, Json)` | one: the array `["a", "b"]` |
| `["a", "b"] \| sink(stdout, Json)` | two: `"a"`, then `"b"` |

- `target` needs a completed value. A stream that may not end is a type
  error under it, and the program bounds the stream first, with `take(n)`.
  `target` does not join a stream itself.
- `sink` writes each element as it arrives.
- In a format whose documents are lines, the two write the same bytes:
  `["a", "b"]` in `Lines` is the lines `a` and `b` under either. The choice
  is then one of timing: `target` holds the whole value before it writes,
  and `sink` feeds the endpoint as values arrive.

### Does a `tap` beside a `source` in one `parallel` block never end?

```hql
r = parallel {
    report = source(analyse, JsonLines)
    sent   = tap(file("frames.jsonl"), JsonLines) | sink(frames, JsonLines)
}
r.report
```

It reads as a block that cannot end: `tap` is the operation that may wait
forever, and `source` in the other branch waits for the child. The block
ends, because the `tap` is of a file that ends.

- `tap` waits forever only on a live endpoint. `file("frames.jsonl")` is a
  `File`, which is `Replayable` and has an end. `tap` of it gives each
  document as a stream and ends at the end of the file.
- `file("frames.jsonl").end` is the live one. `tap` of that follows what
  is appended and does not end.
- When the stream ends, `sink` closes the named pipe. The child reads its
  end of input, writes its report and exits, and `source` returns.

`tap(file(p), F) | sink(…)` runs today and ends: over a file of two
documents it writes two and returns, to standard output and through a
named pipe alike.

The same program with two batches is `source(file("frames.jsonl"),
JsonLines) | target(frames, JsonLines)`. It writes the same bytes. It
holds every frame in memory before the first is written, where `tap` with
`sink` holds one.

### Is `target` implemented as `sink` after a join?

No. They are two functions of `src/extensions/io.rs`, `eval_target` and
`eval_sink`, and neither calls the other. Both are implemented today.

For an endpoint of bytes, a file or standard output:

| | `eval_target` | `eval_sink` |
|---|---|---|
| the input | collected whole into a `Vec` first, when it is still a lazy traversal | iterated, one value at a time |
| the encoding | `format::render` of the whole value, one text | `format::piece` of each value |
| the write | one call, `io::write_whole` | a `Destination` is opened, `line` is called for each value, then `finish` |
| held in memory | every value, then the whole rendered text | one value |

For a `Channel<T>`:

- `eval_target` pushes the input once, as one value: `io::push` of the
  settled input.
- `eval_sink` pushes each element: `io::push` for each.

What follows from the code:

- `target` behaves as `sink` after a join, and is not built that way. It
  collects by itself, in `eval_target` and in `settled` for a channel.
- `sink` streams. It writes each piece as it receives it and calls
  `finish` only when its input ends. That call is what closes the
  endpoint.
- The two share one mechanism in one case. For `file(path).end`,
  `eval_target` opens a `Destination` and writes one `line`, as `sink`
  does for each value.
- The two use different format functions, `render` and `piece`. In `Json`
  that is why `target` writes an array over several lines and `sink`
  writes each document on one line.

### Which operation runs forever?

`tap` of a live endpoint, and nothing else by itself.

| Operation | Ends when | Waits forever |
|---|---|---|
| `source` | the endpoint's data ends. It reads to the end and gives a batch. | not by itself: `source` of a live endpoint, such as `file(path).end`, is a type error |
| `tap` | the endpoint ends | by itself: a live endpoint, a followed file, a socket, a channel or `signals`, may never end |
| `target` | its one document is written | no: it writes one completed value |
| `sink` | its input ends | only when its input does, which means a `tap` is upstream |

`sink` runs forever only when its input does, as in `tap(live) | sink(…)`.
Over a list it writes each element and returns.

### What else can an operation wait for?

An operation other than `tap` waits only for something another process
has to do.

- **`source` waits for an end that the other side supplies.**
  `source(stdin, F)` waits until standard input is closed. `source` of a
  `Command` waits until the child exits, so a child that never exits holds
  it unless `timeout_ms` is set.
- **A named pipe waits on its open.** `tap` and `sink` of a `Fifo` each
  wait until another process opens the other end.
- **A full pipe holds a writer.** `sink` or `target` to a child that has
  stopped reading waits until the child reads again or exits.

A program that hangs is read in that order: a `tap` first, then a read or
a write that waits for another process to close or open something.

### What does `sink` close when it ends?

| Endpoint | When the input of `sink` ends |
|---|---|
| a `Command` | the child's standard input is closed, so the child reads its end of input |
| a named pipe | the pipe is closed, and a reader with no other writer reads its end |
| a file | the file is complete |
| a `Channel<T>` | nothing is closed: a channel has no end |

The channel is the one endpoint where `sink` returning tells the reader
nothing. A child whose standard input is wired to a channel therefore
never reads an end of input, which is the entry "Why does `sort` with
`stdin_from` never answer?" above.
